// Which platforms the Dawn backend opens a window on, and the window each one
// opens.
//
// Dawn draws; it does not own a window. WebGPU takes a surface, and a surface is
// whatever the platform's window system hands out — an `HWND` on Windows, a
// `wl_surface` on a free desktop. Everything else in
// this backend is the same code on every one of them, so the platform split is
// this file and the window declarations it names, and nothing else.
//
// `DawnPlatform` IS the contract. `dawnWindowFor` is total over it and `match`
// has no wildcard arm, so adding a variant here stops the build until that
// platform has a window. A platform cannot be declared supported and left
// unimplemented, which is the property this file exists for.
//
// macOS is absent because there is no `DawnWindowCocoa` yet, and this enum only
// ever lists platforms that have a window. Adding the variant back is what will
// demand the implementation -- which is the point of the list being the contract
// rather than a comment somewhere.
//
// `Platform` is deliberately not reused: it carries `Web` and `Unknown`, which
// have no Dawn window and never will. Narrowing to the platforms that can have
// one keeps every arm below a real implementation rather than a dead branch, and
// puts the one partial step — a host that is none of these — at the single
// boundary in `dawnPlatformFor` instead of in every match.

public enum DawnPlatform {
    Windows
    Linux
}

// The window a Dawn build opens, whatever platform it is opened on.
//
// The members are the whole of what the rest of the backend asks a window for.
// A message pump is not among them on purpose: pumping is where a platform is
// least alike, and what the application layer actually consumes is `AppEvent`,
// which is already platform-neutral. So a window translates its own events and
// the layer above never learns which one it was talking to.
public construct DawnWindow {
    requires {
        // The native handle a surface is made from, or a null pointer when the
        // window did not open.
        function open(title: borrow String, width: Int, height: Int, mode: borrow WindowMode, transparent: Bool, opacity: Float) -> RawPtr
        // The size the surface is configured at, in pixels, and the factor
        // between those pixels and layout points.
        function clientWidth(window: RawPtr) -> Int
        function clientHeight(window: RawPtr) -> Int
        function scale(window: RawPtr) -> Float
        function resize(window: RawPtr, width: Int, height: Int)
        function close(window: RawPtr) -> Void
        // The WebGPU surface for this window.
        //
        // Dawn draws; it does not own a window, and what it takes is whatever
        // the platform's window system hands out -- an `HWND` here, a
        // `wl_display` and `wl_surface` there. Only the window knows which, so
        // only the window can say it, and every other line of this backend is
        // spared the question.
        function createSurface(instance: RawPtr, window: RawPtr) -> RawPtr
        // Draining the platform's event queue is NOT here yet. It belongs here
        // -- it is where two window systems are least alike -- but the Windows
        // pump reads and writes `GraphicsAppRuntimeState` directly rather than
        // answering with the `AppEvent` the application layer actually consumes.
        // Until that is turned around, putting `pump` in this family would only
        // move the entanglement behind a member. It is the next thing to port,
        // and the first thing a second platform will need.
    }
}

// Total over `DawnPlatform`. This is the compile-time check: a new variant
// breaks here, and there is no arm to hide it in.
public function dawnWindowFor(platform: borrow DawnPlatform) -> Any DawnWindow {
    match platform {
        Windows -> return DawnWindowWin32()
        Linux -> return DawnWindowWayland()
    }
}

// The host, narrowed to the platforms a Dawn window exists for.
//
// The narrowing is the one partial step, and it is partial only in principle:
// `validateDawnBackend` refuses every platform this cannot name before a
// backend is chosen, so a host that reaches here is one of these two. A host
// that somehow did not would try to open a Win32 window and fail saying so,
// which is the same answer by a longer road.
public function dawnPlatformForHost() -> DawnPlatform {
    if hostPlatform() == .Linux {
        return .Linux
    }
    return .Windows
}
